  commitment to  advancing stability  in the world through service  to all the parents and loved ones  active duty military members veterans  who are here with us today  i extend a warm greeting on behalf of my  colleagues across the university  and i wish to welcome cadets  liaisons and leaders from the university  of new haven  southern connecticut state university  quinnipiac university and sacred heart  university  we've all participated  we've all anticipated  the day when  public health conditions would  allow us to gather again  for special ceremonies like this one  and i'm so pleased that that day has  come  i'm pleased that for the first time  since 2019 we can honor  the hard work and dedication your hard  work your dedication  of our rotc students  here in person  our ability to celebrate today brings an  extra measure of enthusiasm  to a milestone  day  the sense of service we mark on this  historic occasion dates to the founding  of our institution  and to the republic  let me give you a few examples  in 1701  yale became the first american  university to inscribe service to  society explicitly  in its founding documents  scores of yalies have served with  distinction in every major conflict  since the revolutionary war  yale are gale air force rotc  was one of the original 77 senior air  rotc units established by an order  that general eisenhower signed  and yale was also among the initial  group of universities to establish an  rotc program  more than a century ago  army rotc  in 1917  followed by naval rotc  in 1926  and air force rotc  in 1946  at the time major general robert m  danford predicted the program would  become one of the many priceless  heritages  of future generations  at yale  and i look as i look out  in this amphitheater  i see  that he was right  the 42 air force cadets the 31  midshipmen and the two army cadets  currently participating in yells rotc  program  are carrying forward the proud legacy  of those before them  this includes the valor  of nearly thirty thousand  who served in the first and second world  wars  korea  and vietnam  and the spirit of sacrifice that links  these military men and women is a  hallmark of the spirit  of yale  itself  it is the desire to serve  to look beyond self-interest  it is the desire to contribute  to the common good  and it is in short the desire to improve  the world today  and for future  generations indeed  that is the call of the opening sentence  of our mission statement  this is the call  the class of 2022  has  answered your decision to serve our  country in the armed forces is  especially critical  during this period  of turbulence  we can see  today  the increasing presence of the spirit of  division around the globe  yet today  we've  as we salute your accomplishments  we can also feel  the thriving spirit  of the presence  and opportunity  presence of the opportunity  for service  it's a spirit forged over centuries  indeed the fabric of our nation is woven  by the individuals who have defended it  for nearly 250 years  their resilience their wisdom helped  build a nation and their strength  helped bind it together  during difficult times  their courage help save a world torn  apart by war  twice  their determination has protected  millions from a life of oppression and  hardship  their compassion  has brought food  clothing  shelter  and medicine  to those who need it most  and their unselfish motives in the face  of adversity brought hope  to those who are suffering  throughout  your time at yale  i have seen  the same sense of optimism  courage  and selflessness  in you  and i know the class of 2022  will manage to make us even more proud  in the years  ahead  so now  i'd like to congratulate all our  soon-to-be graduates  and express my appreciation to all our  midshipmen and cadets for their  dedication to service  and their many contributions  to yell  you will soon be second lieutenants and  ensigns  serving as america's ambassadors 